DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The aaccppiiccoonnff utility allows the user control of the ACPI power management
     functions.  The following command-line options are recognized:

     --hh       Displays a summary of available options.

     --ii _b_a_t_t  Get design information and current status of the battery speci‐
              fied by its number, starting with 00.

     --kk _a_c_k   Ack or abort a pending suspend request using the argument pro‐
              vided.  MMoosstt uusseerrss sshhoouulldd nnoott uussee tthhiiss ooppttiioonn ddiirreeccttllyy..

     --ss _t_y_p_e  Enters the specified sleep mode.  Recognized types are 11 (only
              the CPU clock is stopped), 22 (not implemented on most systems
              but similar to S1), 33 (the CPU context is lost and memory con‐
              text is preserved), and 44 (the CPU context is lost and memory
              context is stored to disk).  Sleep states may also be given as
              S1, S2, etc.  The supported states depend on BIOS implementa‐
              tion, including ACPI byte code (AML).  If the _/_e_t_c_/_r_c_._s_u_s_p_e_n_d
              and _/_e_t_c_/_r_c_._r_e_s_u_m_e scripts are executable, they will be run by
              devd(8) or apmd(8) before and after entering the given sleep
              state.

              The _/_e_t_c_/_r_c_._s_u_s_p_e_n_d and _/_e_t_c_/_r_c_._r_e_s_u_m_e scripts use the
              rcorder(8) utility to call scripts in _/_e_t_c_/_r_c_._d_/ and the
              _$_l_o_c_a_l___s_t_a_r_t_u_p directories that have a "suspend" or "resume"
              KEYWORD, respectively.  Called scripts are supplied with a sin‐
              gle "suspend" / "resume" command line argument.  See rc.conf(5)
              for more information about _$_l_o_c_a_l___s_t_a_r_t_u_p.

